# Last.fm API credentials for scrobbling (G6b).
#
# Copy this file to `lastfm.properties` in the project root and fill both values
# in. That file is untracked - see .gitignore - and this template must never
# carry a real credential. The build fails if it does.
#
# Both values are issued together at https://www.last.fm/api/account/create.
#
# Do NOT assume a format. Last.fm's specification documents the api key's length
# ("Your 32-character API Key", /api/authspec §3.3, §4.1, §7) but not its
# character set, and documents neither for the shared secret - §8's own worked
# example uses a secret of 'ilovecher'. The "32-character hexadecimal" wording in
# the spec describes api_sig, which is an md5 output. So nothing here validates
# the shape of either value: an absent or unedited-placeholder value means the
# feature is off, and anything else is passed to Last.fm to judge.
#
#   LASTFM_API_KEY     identifies this application. Public by design.
#
#   LASTFM_API_SECRET  the shared secret. It signs every authenticated call
#                      (auth.getToken, auth.getSession, track.updateNowPlaying,
#                      track.scrobble): there is no unsigned path, so it has to
#                      be reachable from the device.
#
# ## The secret ships in the APK, and that is the accepted trade
#
# This is the model Last.fm's own desktop/mobile authentication assumes, and it
# means the secret is EXTRACTABLE from a released build - `strings` on the APK
# is enough, and R8 does not obscure string constants. The owner accepted this
# for v1 (option A). What that exposure does and does not allow:
#
#   it does NOT expose any listener's account. Reading or writing a profile
#   needs a per-user session key, which is obtained on the device through
#   Last.fm's own web authorisation and never leaves it.
#
#   it DOES let someone make signed calls as this application. The realistic
#   worst case is abuse leading Last.fm to suspend the key (API error 26),
#   which disables scrobbling for every listener until a new key is issued.
#
# So this is an availability risk, not a user-data one. If that ever stops
# being acceptable, the fix is already scaffolded: all signing goes through the
# `LastfmSigner` seam, and a server-side signer is a second implementation of
# it rather than a rewrite.
#
# ## Never put anything else in this file
#
# In particular never a listener's password or session key. A session key is
# per-user runtime state that belongs in app storage on the device, not in a
# build input. The build refuses a file that looks like it holds one.
#
# Absent or empty is a supported state, and it is the state CI and every fresh
# clone build in: `LastfmConfig.isConfigured` is then false, and the app has no
# Last.fm in it at all. See LastfmConfig.
LASTFM_API_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_LASTFM_API_KEY
LASTFM_API_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_LASTFM_SHARED_SECRET
